  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nHigh pressure shifts northeast tonight as low pressure moves north \nfrom out of Florida. Dry weather continues through the night. \nLowered overnight lows once again as NBM is way too warm, as seen \nover the last few nights. Previously, we were expecting dry \nconditions to continue across the area on Saturday. However, the low \nthat will be moving through NC/VA has shifted slightly further west. \nThis means that the western edge of the precip shield associated \nwith this system will now be nudging into our area. NBM has slight \nchance and chance POPs entering portions of the east TN mountains, \nnortheast TN, and southwest VA by Saturday night. During the day \ntime hours though, mostly dry conditions are expected along with \ncontinued low RH values. Additionally, because of the westward shift \nof the low, breezier winds are now expected across the higher \nelevations of the east TN mountains, northeast TN, and southwest VA. \nThis will lead to a slight increase in elevated fire weather \nconcerns prior to any rainfall that moves in Saturday night. \nRainfall amounts will generally be around 0.10 inches or less for \nmost areas, some areas may see up to 0.25 inches (just for the \nareas already mentioned that are expected to see the rain). \n\nA few rain showers will remain into Sunday before the low pulls off \nto the northeast Sunday evening. If the track pushes even further \nwest, additional areas (further west into the east TN Valley) may \nsee rainfall Saturday night into Sunday.\n\nA weak front moves in Sunday night and brings additional chances for \nlight rain into portions of the area. Most areas stay dry, but for \nthose who do get rain, amounts will generally only be a few \nhundredths of an inch.\n\n\nCooler temps and dry weather expected Monday through Tuesday behind \nthe frontal passage with temperatures back down closer to seasonal \nnormals. Our next chance of precip comes Wed/Thu as an upper trough \napproaches from the northwest and a system in the Gulf approaches \nfrom the south.",
